    <h2>FAQ</h2>

    <h3>Is Stripe better than PayPal for online businesses?</h3>
    <p>Stripe is better for online businesses that need deep developer control, custom checkout flows, subscriptions, and multi-currency support. Its API is widely considered the best in the payments industry, with excellent documentation and SDKs for every major language. PayPal is better for businesses that want fast setup, consumer trust, and access to a wallet with over 400 million active users. For a developer-led online business, Stripe wins. For a business that prioritizes consumer familiarity and quick integration, PayPal is the stronger choice.</p>

    <h3>Which is cheaper, Stripe or PayPal?</h3>
    <p>Stripe and PayPal have identical standard pricing at 2.9% plus 30 cents per domestic card transaction. However, the total cost differs based on how you use them. Stripe charges no additional monthly fee and includes features like subscriptions, invoicing, and fraud protection in the base rate. PayPal charges the same 2.9% plus 30 cents for card payments but adds fees for some features like recurring billing and chargeback protection. For international transactions, Stripe charges an additional 1.5% and PayPal charges up to 1.5% plus a cross-border fee. For most domestic businesses, the cost is comparable.</p>

    <h3>Which has better developer tools, Stripe or PayPal?</h3>
    <p>Stripe has significantly better developer tools. Its API documentation is considered the gold standard in the payments industry, with interactive examples, SDKs for every major language, and a dashboard that lets you test in a sandbox with real-looking data. Stripe also offers Stripe CLI for local testing, webhooks with retry logic, and libraries like Stripe.js and Elements for custom checkout. PayPal's developer tools are functional but less polished, with documentation that is harder to navigate and a sandbox that is slower. For developers, Stripe is the clear winner.</p>

    <h3>Can I use Stripe and PayPal together?</h3>
    <p>Yes, and most online businesses do. A common setup is to use Stripe as the primary payment gateway for credit and debit cards, and offer PayPal as an alternative payment method at checkout. This gives customers flexibility and can increase conversion rates by 10 to 20 percent, because some shoppers prefer PayPal for its buyer protection and one-click checkout. Most ecommerce platforms like Shopify, WooCommerce, and BigCommerce support both Stripe and PayPal simultaneously, with no conflict.</p>

    <h3>Which has better global reach, Stripe or PayPal?</h3>
    <p>PayPal has broader consumer reach, with over 400 million active accounts in more than 200 countries. Consumers trust PayPal and many prefer it for international purchases because of its buyer protection. Stripe is available in over 46 countries for businesses but supports accepting payments from customers worldwide. For consumer reach and trust, PayPal wins. For business availability and multi-currency processing with local payment methods, Stripe offers more granular control and supports over 135 currencies. For a global business, using both is the best approach.</p>

    <h3>Which is better for subscriptions, Stripe or PayPal?</h3>
    <p>Stripe is better for subscriptions because its billing engine is more flexible and developer-friendly. Stripe Billing supports tiered pricing, usage-based billing, free trials, prorations, add-ons, and smart retries for failed payments. The API lets you build custom subscription flows that fit any business model. PayPal supports recurring payments and subscriptions, but the features are less flexible, with limited support for usage-based billing and prorations. For SaaS and subscription businesses, Stripe is the industry standard.</p>

